    "lawCode": "Amd §§20.20, 60.27 & 80.10, add Art 122 §§122.00 - 122.15, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3314\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to crimes involving the death\nor injury of a worker\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill seeks to protect workers from employers and supervisors that\nignore, disregard or fail to comply with safety protocols by amending\nthe penal code to create a new Article 122 of the Penal Law - Workplace\nSafety Offenses.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1 - amends section 20.20 of the penal law relating to criminal liabil-\nity of corporations by adding a new Article 122 of the penal law - work-\nplace safety offenses - endangering the welfare of a workers in the 1st,\n2nd, or 3rd degree for which an agent of a corporation could be guilty.\n\n§ 2 - amends section a and b of subdivision 1 of section 80.10 relating\nto fines on corporation by adding fines for violations under new article\n122 of the Penal law. For felony offenses, the fine may be up to\n$50,000. For misdemeanors offenses the fine may be up to $25,000.\n\n§ 3 - Creates a new Article 122 of the Penal Law - Workplace Safety\nOffenses\n\n§ 122.00 - endangering the welfare of a worker in the third degree - if\nan employer or supervisor ignores, disregards or fails to comply with\nworkplace safety protocols or procedures; and that conduct exposes a\nworker to bodily injury - punishable as a class A misdemeanor.\n\n§ 122.05 - endangering the welfare of a worker in the second degree - if\nan employer or supervisor ignores, disregards or fails to comply with\nworkplace safety protocols or procedures, and that conduct exposes a\nworker to serious physical injury - punishable as a class E felony.\n\n§ 122.10 - endangering the welfare of a worker in the first degree - if\nan employer or supervisor ignores, disregards or fails to comply with\nworkplace safety protocols or procedures, and that contributes directly\nto the death of a worker - punishable as a class D felony.\n\n§ 4 - effective date of 30 days after signed into law\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWorkplace deaths and serious injuries continue to be commonplace in the\nconstruction industry. Of the more than 400,000 workplace fatalities\nsince Congress enacted the Occupational Safety and Health Act (OSHA),\nfewer than 80 have been prosecuted, and only about a dozen lead to\nconvictions. That is roughly one conviction for every 33,000 fatalities.\nIn the few cases that have resulted in conviction, the penalty was only\n$1,000 on average. Under the OSHA, the criminal penalty is a Class B\nmisdemeanor, and carries, at most, up to 6 months imprisonment. The\nweakness of OSHA's punitive measures has therefore failed to encourage\nsafer work environments.\nThis bill would strengthen New York State's laws by creating a new Arti-\ncle 122 of the Penal law of Workplace Safety Offenses increasing both\nfines and penalties when an employer or supervisor ignores, disregards\nor fails to comply with workplace safety protocols or procedures, and\nthat contributes directly to bodily injury, serious physical injury of\nthe death of a worker.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2016 Session - New bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n30 days after being signed into law.\n",
